# TDVP K230 diagnostics

`tdvp-diagnostics` is an r10 installation profile. It does not bundle copied
executables, system libraries, SDK files, or firmware utilities. Instead it
brings independently versioned packages through exact dependencies:

* `strace` for focused system-call traces;
* `htop` for interactive process inspection without the optional `libcap`
  feature;
* `lsof` for Linux `/proc` open-file and socket inspection without `libtirpc`.
  Its full implementation is invoked as `tdvp-lsof`, preserving K230's
  immutable BusyBox `/usr/bin/lsof` applet;
  and
* `iperf3` for controlled-LAN TCP throughput and UDP-loss measurements without
  its optional OpenSSL authentication mode; and
* `netcat` for controlled TCP/UDP listener and connection checks.

The profile's own payload is only this document. Uninstalling it never removes
one of those tools if another installed package still depends on it. Some
diagnostics are intentionally constrained by device privileges: `tdvp-lsof` may
only reveal processes the current user can inspect, and packet/network tests
must run against a controlled endpoint rather than an untrusted public server.

After installing an unsigned r10 candidate on a real K230 device, verify:

    strace true
    htop --version
    tdvp-lsof --version
    tdvp-lsof /tmp
    tdvp-lsof -i
    iperf3 --version
    nc -h
    # Use `nc -l` only on a controlled LAN endpoint.
    # On a controlled LAN: start `iperf3 -s` on one endpoint, then run:
    iperf3 -c <controlled-server>
    iperf3 -u -c <controlled-server>

Record installation, normal operation, uninstall, and rollback results before
promoting any of these candidate IPKs to an immutable public feed release.
